"The Java type a value of a graph's scalar arrives as, from both populations that answer. The engine's own scalars are a closed list and carry the type their coercion produces. A consumer's scalar names a constant, and what that constant coerces to is a reading of the classpath rather than anything the name says, which is why the constant named here is one a consumer really names: sakila-example's schema spells this same one."
scalar LocalDate @scalarType(scalar: "graphql.scalars.ExtendedScalars.Date")

"Declined: a scalar the author declared and left unbound reaches no Java type at all."
scalar Unbound

"Declined: the spelling is not itself an answer, so a reference no constant matches resolves to nothing."
scalar Missing @scalarType(scalar: "graphql.scalars.ExtendedScalars.NoSuchConstant")

type Film @table(name: "film") {
  filmId: ID
  released: LocalDate
  title: String
}

extend type Query {
  films: [Film!]!
}

# What the reading found in the jar, which is the fact the resolution below stands on. Contains
# rather than equals: the artifact declares a great many constants and this document is about one.
extend schema @assertContains(relation: "code_scalar_constant", rows: """
  class_name,                    field_name, input_type
  graphql.scalars.ExtendedScalars, Date,     java.time.LocalDate
  """)

# And the model's side: the graph's own scalars resolved, engine and consumer alike. The five
# spec built-ins answer whether or not the document uses them, the engine arm reading a closed
# list rather than the schema; the two declines above add nothing, which is the point of
# claiming the whole population rather than listing what was expected to appear.
extend schema @expectEquals(relation: "intent_scalar_java_type", rows: """
  type_name, java_type
  Boolean,   java.lang.Boolean
  Float,     java.lang.Double
  ID,        java.lang.String
  Int,       java.lang.Integer
  LocalDate, java.time.LocalDate
  String,    java.lang.String
  """)
